A||N||13||jlruiz67||Engine Conversions||||4||
Z||000000||jlruiz67||07-01-2000||11:44 AM||||Hello all<BR>I noticed that there are many Fieros in this forum with engine conversions of all kinds and like to know if people that have performed the conversions could post "Detailed" instructions on this forum. I like to convert my 200,000 mile 87 GT but I don't want to invest on a kit and I like to know if it can be done. I noticed that for very detailed information I have to spend money. I like to know if you can share with us the parts list, modifications, specifications,pictures, rate the level of dificulty, what you would do diferent,what kind of conversion and anything else you can think of. I know that there are kits available but after looking at what is included I find them to expencive. I understand that there is only a small market for resale but I like to know if anyone can share there know how in detail and give all of us that are on a tight budget the oportunity to keep our Fieros on the road. Most conversion kits cost more than a good Fiero, The V8 conversion cost more than the Engine plus a good Fiero. I know supply and demand, but we are among friends and friends share. When I do my conversion (3.4, 3800 or V8) I will post every detail including diagrams for modifications, but that wont happend till next year. If you have converted your Fiero and have detail information, Could you share? If I go with the 3.4 conversion, I have to drill new starter holes and like to know how I would do that (templates, location,etc) and what other mods are required including parts list. If I go with the V8 or 3800, I know that I have to get the computer of the donor and fab new engine mounts,etc. The only conversion that I see extreme detail is the grand am brake upgrade, I can buy parts at my local autoshop and do it. But when it comes to engine conversion no one is giving as much detail (specialy on V8 conversions) as to be able to do it without a kit. Some one Quoted me 13,000 to put a northstar, others charge close to 3,000 for a kit that doesn't include all and I mean all the part needed for the conversion (not including worn parts that need to be replaced) I mean for the love of god and fellow Fiero Maniac could some on brake the code of silence and give up the secrets? I was going to buy a conversion kit just so I could get all the details and post them, but I can't just return the kit and get my money back so the next best thing is for you to post your experience and all the details you might have.<P>"FIERO OWNERS NEED YOU TO STEP UP THE PLATE AND SAVE US SOME CASH AND BRING LIFE BACK TO OUR AGING FIEROS"<P>Thank you very much and hope to see some good stuff soon.||||reg||4
Z||000001||chris west||07-01-2000||12:31 PM||||Have you checked out West Coast FIERO'S web it has a lot of info. about engine conversions. at cwestco.com||||reg||10
Z||000002||jlruiz67||07-01-2000||12:59 PM||||Your website is very informative but it does not include detail instructions on how to upgrade your fiero without a kit and at a low price. Not to say that your site is bad, you run a buisness and as they go you are up there in informing the costumer and you have a good selection of products, I really liked the section on the battery relocation, you give it all up and show us how to install the box in detail. I know that you don't have the time and you don't want to loose cash by giving up all the information that its required to do a conversion in my home with out a kit and thats what I want to do. I did notice that you have a lot of parts that I will be needing for my conversion in stock and by next year you will here more of me when I go to your shop and buy all the things that I need. I live in Laguna Niguel CA. Could you tell me the hours and days your open so I can stop buy and visit (shop). I'm on a tight budget but my fiero has 200,000 miles on it and if I want to keep it on the road I will have to upgrade the power plant soon.<P>P.S. in the engine conversion section you have links for "what it takes" could you tell me if that's all it takes to complete a engine conversion? And if so is there a detailed parts list that you can provide. I also noticed that you don't have a straight 3.4 conversion.<p>[This message has been edited by jlruiz67 (edited 07-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||phillip||07-01-2000||01:15 PM||||hate to believe anyone's "holding back" info. most posts i read are various folks trying to describe whats been done or sharing bits of info they've found.<P> in a post last week i mentioned a guy walking up to me and telling me he could put a v8 in my car for $500 plus the cost of the engine, and/or he could get close to double the hp out of either stock engine. i didn't believe him but now i wish i would have at least found out how to get in contact with him again. i'm looking for him.<P>i think part of the problem is that while a lot of us would like to do this few of us actually have and among those are the ones who had it done for them and can only go so deep into the technical aspects of it. it's not like your common camaro swap. if it were i have a cousin who could do it probaly over a weekend in his back yard (probaly can any way but like you said, some details are needed). plus with a fiero being a fairly new car (i mean when you talk about swaps ) and having a small engine to start with emmissions have to dealt with (for me anyway).<P>actually in spite the fact that his intrances to the forum are usually to fight ( i wish that weren't so), v8 archie has a pretty thourough out line of the v8 swap on his web pages. you still need the kit though. if nothing else you need the adapter plate.<P>another problem is that although this forum is a great info sourse, most of us are still miles apart from each other. i'd like to think if some one had a nice swap worked out and you lived right around the corner they'd let you take a look at it and try to help out with details. the best that can be done is pics (if you have that ability) and what ever description the owner is capable of.<P>if you've been on the forum very long you've seen that each engine that has potential has been discussed about as much as possible. remember the bish (he out there? hoax maybe?) post (that was almost holy grail like). wouldn't you like to get a look at that drive train in person?<P>whew! long post and all to say you're just gonna have to get your back into it and glean through chaff with the rest of us.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||07-01-2000||07:50 PM||||Frankly, by the time we finish the swap, we're too tired and boggled to write a book on the subject.<P>You need to decide on which swap you want to do and then the people that have done it can come out of the woodwork. But expecting everyone to jump in at one time is cacaphony of information.<P>IMHO the order of easy to hard is:<P>3.1L upgrade (crank and pistons).<BR>3.4O upgrade (long block)<BR>3.8L NA or SC with 4T60E trans<BR>3.8L NA or SC with 4T65E trans<BR>Any 3.8L NA or SC with a Getrag<BR>3.4L DOHC with Getrag or 4T60E trans<BR>Quad 4 with any trans<BR>4.0L or 4.6L Norstar with 4T80E tran<BR>Any V8.<P>I've done 3.8L SC swaps into A cars and I'm in the middle of a DOHC swap into Fiero. That's my experience limit.||||reg||1
Z||000005||phillip||07-02-2000||12:20 AM||||terry, are you using some kit for the twin cam? did you have to change trannies or use what it had (and is it stick or manual)?did you start with a 4 or 6 cyl. fiero? i would be starting with a 4cyl. manual '87. if i were able and could choose the twin cam would be the 6 cyl. of choice to me, even over a sc3800.<P>what makes it harder than the 3800 to you?<p>[This message has been edited by phillip (edited 07-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||tiacPaul||07-02-2000||03:01 AM||||Of all of these engines that are being thrown out i haven't heard the one I was thinking of.  I have an oppertunity to buy an entire buick with a 3300 series engine for $600 because of a bent chasis.  It looks straight foward, like the 2.8, and is the three speed trans the same as mine?  Do many of these conversions require a new fuel pump?  The engine seams great but I just wanted to get some feedback before I try it.||||reg||5
Z||000007||mrfixit58||07-02-2000||12:24 PM||||I thought about this one as well because they are so plentiful and CHEAP. The 3300 is the same blcck as the 3800. So, the cost for the conversion would be about the same. Unfortunatly, you won't gain much performance over the 2.8. The numbers just don't add up... hp/$$$.<P>Roy||||reg||4
Z||000008||88formula||07-02-2000||02:20 PM||||The 3300 engine puts out 165 HP. It’s not worth the trouble in my mind. Either spend the money and get a later model 3800 engine with 205 HP, modify your 2.8 and get 165 HP, or get a 3.4 engine that will put out 180-200 HP with some modifications like a hotter cam. Plus the 3.4 swap is the easiest to. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||07-03-2000||02:21 AM||||I agree, the 3300 is wash against a nice 3.1L or 3.4L block change.<P>Phillip, <P>No, I don't use a kits from anyone. On the DOHC, I'm using a Beretta Getrag with the needed shifter mods although the 4spd is really all it needs (95MPH in 3rd vs 89MPH for the Getrag is the only real benefit to me). The 4spd just makes the swap a bit simpler. I'm in California so I have to add the air injection tubes and pump. <P>I started with a 2.8L V6. <P>Both the 3800SC and the DOHC have issues. <BR>1. Both need new exhausts. <BR>2. The DOHC can use Fiero mounts, the 3800SC takes some fabrication. <BR>3. The 3800SC requires a custom flywheel, the DOHC doesn't. <BR>4. The DOHC requires that the right hood hinge be cut to clear the cams. <BR>5. The DOHC rear plugs can't be removed without removing the hood or dropping the cradle a bit, the 3800SC is easy. <BR>6. Both need new AC compressors and custom lines. <BR>7. If you use the 4T60E trans, you will have to fabricate new axles since the Fiero uses smaller axle assemblies.<BR>8. There is no practical way to handle the dogbone. Both need thought.<P>Frankly I would consider the 3800 swap before the DOHC, I just got the DOHC for a reasonable price. <P>The DOHC is not a street drag engine. It's strength is not off the line, but once you are moving. <P>I would also consider using the 4T60E that comes with the 3800SC's before a Getrag. The ECM likes the 4T60E being there. I like the 4T60E (or 4T65E if you are doing a 97 and up swap.)<P>I prefer the 94/95 since you can run the 92/93 pulley and get about the same power as the 96 and up SC's. I can also change the calibrations on the 92/95, but not on the 96 and up. It's far harder. Even a 92/93 SC is very nice! You can add the 94/95 blower later (after your pocketbook recovers).<P>What makes the DOHC harder? It don't fit.<P>Swapping any engine into the Fiero is not for the weak at heart. The SC swap into the Ciera GT was a cakewalk in comparison.<BR>&lt;breath&gt;<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||BAM-BAM||07-03-2000||11:55 AM||||  Here you go, try this. It's about as cheap as you are going to get. <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=371862730" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=371862730</A> <P>  Late, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT||||reg||12
